{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'function levelText' -A24 .cache/engine-src/labs/playground/levels/plan.ts\nrg -n 'format|version' engine/world/level/parse.ts engine/world/level/*.ts | head -25\"","aggregated_output":"91:export function levelText(header: Readonly<Record<string, string | number>>, storeys: readonly StoreyBlock[]): string {\n92-  const block = (keys: Readonly<Record<string, string | number>>) => `---\\n${Object.entries(keys).map(([key, value]) => `${key}: ${value}`).join('\\n')}\\n---\\n`; // prettier-ignore\n93-  return block(header) + storeys.map((storey) => `${block(storey.keys)}${storey.plan.map}\\n`).join('');\n94-}\nengine/world/level/level.ts:40:      \"The level's text: optional front matter, then the map (engine/world/level/parse.ts gives the format).\",\nengine/world/level/legend.ts:9: * storeys (format 2): void `~`, the door `+`, the window `:`, the railing `!`, the flight of stairs `<` up to the\nengine/world/level/legend.ts:166:/** The v4 glyphs by id (WP 13.13): what levels written as storeys (format 2) add. */\nengine/world/level/heights.ts:8: * surface, and `roomAt`. The 2D queries read one storey: a format-1 level, a format-2 level's ground storey, or any\nengine/world/level/heights.ts:120:/** Each storey of `level` as a height source, bottom first: a format-1 level is one, standing on 0. */\nengine/world/level/heights.ts:160: * (outside the ground slab). For a format-1 level as for one written as storeys.\nengine/world/level/heights.ts:173: * `y`; 0 below them all, and in a format-1 level, which is one storey.\nengine/world/level/heights.ts:182: * The storey of the surface `floorBelow` finds at (x, y, z), an index into `level.storeys` (0 in a format-1 level): a\nengine/world/level/glyph.ts:6: * levels written as storeys (format 2), void, full height, an opening (a door or a window), a head and a room marker\nengine/world/level/glyph.ts:7: * (`STOREY_FIELDS`, which a format-1 level's legend leaves out). The level compiler (engine/world/level/compile.ts)\nengine/world/level/glyph.ts:76:      \"How its floor runs: 'flat' at floor; 'ramp' rising in a straight slope from floor to top across each filled rectangle of its tiles, toward the one side whose neighbours stand at top (stairs up to a gallery); 'dais' an octagonal platform on each square block of its tiles, its edge sloping from floor to top; 'flight' (format 2) stairs rising from floor to the floor of the storey above, toward the one side that floor meets.\",\nengine/world/level/glyph.ts:162:      \"Format 2: its box rises from its storey's floor to the floor of the storey above (or to the roof over it), whatever its top: a wall, a pillar. A format-1 level reads its top.\",\nengine/world/level/glyph.ts:187:/** The fields only format 2 reads: a format-1 level's legend leaves them out (engine/world/level/compile.ts). */\nengine/world/level/glyph.ts:234:/** The problems of a glyph's format-2 fields: void, full, an opening, a head and a room marker. */\nengine/world/level/compile.ts:6: * mesh descriptors, spawn points and light spots. A level written as storeys (format 2) compiles through storeys.ts to\nengine/world/level/compile.ts:17: * order). It is frozen all the way down, shares nothing a caller can change, and holds only canonical data. A format-1\nengine/world/level/compile.ts:19: * did before format 2 existed.\nengine/world/level/compile.ts:22: * dais stands on the dais and a torch spot on a brazier sits above its bowl. In format 2 a light names its room, its\nengine/world/level/compile.ts:77:/** One glyph as a format-1 level holds it: every field of its entry but `kind` and those only storeys read. */\nengine/world/level/compile.ts:134:    format: LEVEL_FORMAT,\nengine/world/level/compile.ts:292: * A checked format-2 level compiled into plain data, as storeys.ts's file comment describes: its storeys' grids, ramps,\nengine/world/level/compile.ts:327:    format: LE