{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_150","type":"agent_message","text":"The packaged game now boots and renders in Chromium without errors. I’ve inspected the title over the live world. The prologue trace also found that fire could reach the player during the healing lesson, so I moved the opening fireline to give that lesson room.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_151","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > scripts/write-interview.mjs <<'EOF'\n/** @file Writes the required plain-text exit interview and catalogue record, also embedded by the bundler. */\nimport {writeFileSync,readFileSync} from 'node:fs';\nconst lock=JSON.parse(readFileSync('engine-lock.json','utf8'));\nconst data={\n sourceInformation:{date:'2026-11-05 07:25:16',source:'Far Cry 2',plan:'PLAN.md',genre:'Open-world shooter with systemic fire and survival pressure',perspective:'First person, with shoulder, driving chase and eye-level body cameras',tech:'my-3dge at ea717c7; TypeScript 5.9.3; three.js 0.182.0 on WebGPU; Rapier 0.21.0; HTML/CSS; Vite 7.3.7; Vitest 4.1.12; Playwright 1.64.1 with installed Chromium 153 and WebKit 26.6',artDirection:'Native low-poly style, sun-bleached earth, procedural savanna, original props, weapons and articulated characters',animation:'Native procedural humanoid and action layers, authored medical and field actions, visible gun reload poses, suspension and buoyancy',audio:'Code-only tracker music, synthesized effects and formant gibberish voices; music defaults to 10%',online:'AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0; host-sequenced shared-world co-op for 1–4; standalone BroadcastChannel rooms and WebRTC invite codes',testing:'Headless simulation, scripted campaign playthrough, replay/capture, multiplayer determinism, browser UI and protocol checks. Real GPUs, physical controllers, speakers, cross-machine networking and iOS Safari are unverified.',research:'Read PLAN.md, the my-3dge guide, public API and engine examples, and the AI Hub session protocol and harness source.',environment:'Codex CLI (headless); Linux',model:'OpenAI, gpt-6.1-sol, reasoning effort max',usage:'added on import'},\n testingFeedback:[\n  'The confirmed issue count is recorded in the development issue log; the final verification report records the passing checks.',\n  'Startup, capture, undefined NPC input and GPU warm-up failures were game breaking. The issue log identifies each confirmed case.',\n  'The scripted campaign and capture/restore tests were most helpful: they exposed state ownership, tutorial hazards and interaction ordering.',\n  'The first long high-quality GPU startup wait was least helpful. Geometry assembly and smaller targeted probes made the cause clear. Real hardware and speakers remain unverified.'\n ],\n engineFeedback:[\n  'The deterministic CPU simulation and native procedural animation made systemic fire and shared-world play practical.',\n  'Yes. Setup queues spawns, schemas reject reserved fields, and captures reject shared or undefined component data. Integration probes identified those mistakes.',\n  'The shared input path and game systems: all-seat firearms, fire, survival, contracts, vehicles, saves and lockstep networking.',\n  'First-person arms are a closed rig, and eagerly warming a batch for each gun part and variant made startup expensive. Assembled gun meshes and visible pose variants work around both.',\n  'None. The pinned engine remains unchanged.',\n  'Expose first-person hand/action poses and an asset warm-up API that can request only the models a game needs.'\n ],\n promptFeedback:[\n  'The source-mechanics brief and explicit delivery, controller, audio and test requirements gave the game a clear identity.',\n  'PLAN.md mixed firm feature requirements with starting values and verified implementation recipes. Some recipes needed adjustment for the pinned engine.',\n  'Making survival, story, vehicles, fire, UI and co-op behave consistently in one deterministic world was the hardest part.',\n  'Separate feature acceptance from suggested implementation recipes, and provide a compact machine-readable feature checklist alongside the narrative plan.'\n ],\n aiHub:{name:'Bar Cry',tagline:'Everything here has a price.',description:'Carry a warrant into a dry-season war, with a rusting rifle, a fever and a ledger of debts. Wind-driven fire, fragile weapons, drivable pickups and boats, and a cooperative depot raid share one open world.',type:'Game',genre:'Shooter',source:'Far Cry 2',series:'Far Cry',players:'1–4',controls:'WASD / left stick moves; mouse / right stick looks; click / R2 fires; E / Square uses; R / hold Square reloads; H / L1 heals; M / D-pad up maps; Esc / Options pauses.',tags:['3d','first-person','co-op','vehicles','exploration','low-poly']}\n};\nwriteFileSync('../exit-interview.json',JSON.stringify(data,null,2)+'\\\\n');\nJSON.parse(readFileSync('../exit-interview.json','utf8'));\nEOF\nnode scripts/write-interview.mjs\ncat > ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md <<'EOF'\n# Engine feedback\n\nEngine pinned at my-3dge ea717c7. No local engine patches.\n\n- Reproducible CPU rules, captures and native actions are strong foundations for a systemic shooter and host-sequenced co-op.\n- Scene setup queues spawns too: querying a just-spawned campaign inside setup returned no entity. Assemble the initial state locally, then spawn it, and begin rules after setup.\n- Capture checks reject shared arrays and undefined object properties. Cloning each seat's buttons and omitting absent NPC aim fields fixed both issues. Hashes alone did not reveal this as clearly as captures did.\n- \"'`createPropMeshes` eagerly builds every registered prop. Twelve guns × three tiers × four poses × multiple parts produced over two thousand geometry objects during warm-up and exhausted software WebGPU memory. Keeping the authored shapes but merging each gun variant into one native prop batch allowed the packaged world to boot.\n- The closed first-person hand rig has no game-authored medical or reload action entry. Visible gun pose variants handle reloads; custom eye-level body cameras show authored medical actions.\n- `addWeapons`, melee and interactions apply only to the local player. The game supplies systems driven by each seat, preserving native weapons components and shot events for presentation.\n- Desirable API changes: first-person action poses; selective asset warm-up; explicit all-seat input support; a small game save format independent of full captures.\nEOF'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
